{
  "key": "pioneer_8",
  "name": "Pioneer 8",
  "rocket": "Thrust Augmented Improved Delta (Delta E-1)",
  "payload": "Pioneer C solar-orbit probe (65 kg) with magnetometer, plasma, cosmic-ray, cosmic-dust and electric-field instruments",
  "status": "Success",
  "destinationLabel": "Solar orbit (0.99-1.09 AU)",
  "significance": "Pioneer 8 joined Pioneers 6 and 7 in orbit around the Sun, launched ahead of Earth so it drifted outward and added a third vantage point for watching the solar wind. On 18 January 1968 the Sun, Earth and Pioneer 8 lined up, letting it look down the length of Earth's magnetic tail, and by mid-1968 its data suggested the tail was shorter than the 320 million km some theories predicted. It carried the first cosmic-dust detector on the Pioneers, and NASA's improved Deep Space Network receivers let all three probes be heard over much greater distances.",
  "statusNote": "PATH IS CALCULATED, NOT MEASURED (drawn dashed): there is no ephemeris for the mission years, so the orbit is built from its published size (perihelion and aphelion, inclination) and pinned to Earth's position at launch, assuming the craft left Earth along Earth's direction of travel (so launch is the orbit's perihelion). Two-body motion, sampled every 30 days (tools/build_solar_orbit_h.py). Published orbit: 0.99 x 1.09 AU, 387.50 days, inclination 0.057 deg (NSSDCA); Siddiqi prints it as \"1.0080 x 0.9892 AU\", a misprint (NSSDCA's figures and the size of Horizons' 1997 orbit, semi-major axis 1.04 AU, both point to about 0.99 x 1.09). The calculated orbit is 0.984 x 1.090 AU, 385.8 days, and puts the craft on the Sun-Earth line, anti-sunward of Earth, on 1968-01-18, matching the alignment the mission record gives for that day. Drawn from launch (1967-12-13 14:08 UT, Cape Kennedy) for two orbits, to 1970-01-01; contact continued for nearly 30 years (last reacquired 1996-08-22, Siddiqi). JPL Horizons (-20) has a trajectory only for 1997-07-01 to 1999-12-30; run back on two-body motion it misses Earth at launch by 0.12 AU, so it isn't used for the 1960s path.",
